## Summary

Audited the codebase against its documented trust model, including authentication, authorization, system writes, public routes, invitations, content access, webhooks and external fetches. Found and fixed a high-severity resource-exhaustion issue reachable through ordinary signed-in member writes: overlapping, acyclic group rules caused repeated recursive expansion outside the query visit budget.

Before: overlapping group rules repeatedly recalculated shared groups, producing exponential CPU and allocation work during membership queries, permission checks, warming and previews.
Now: one membership resolver per calculation reuses completed group results across recursive expansion and draft rules, preserving membership reasons, permissions and dependency tracking.

The 59-write, 18-level reproduction allocated 371 MB before the fix and about 22 KB afterward; local resolution time fell from 367 ms to 53 microseconds. Allocation growth is the primary evidence. The fix uses temporary result maps and introduces no rule limits, policy change, flags, schema changes or production test hooks. Regression tests create and query the graph as an ordinary member and check membership updates after base membership changes.

All 467 database tests passed in two disjoint runs under the required 30-second cap; the combined run exceeded that cap. Auth, MCP, tools and Ask tests, go vet ./..., and diff checks passed. Independent review completed with zero findings. Main merged automatically without conflicts or manual resolutions; the two security regressions, draft preview and randomized effective-membership invalidation test passed again after integration. The merge was left uncommitted for byre to conclude.

Deliberately preserved public share previews and privileged-administrator request logging as documented trusted behavior. Other investigated concerns did not establish another high-value fix. No production writes, deployment or GitHub issue creation occurred.

