Goal and scope:
Audit the entire codebase against its documented trust model, substantiate reachable security problems, and fix the few highest-value issues. Work was performed solo, as explicitly required. Read `AGENTS.md`, architecture/trust documentation and prior security findings. Investigated authentication/session/OAuth boundaries, host routing, app-admin grants, system writes, public invitations/feeds, content/blob permissions, webhook authentication, fetch/upload paths, browser rendering and paid-operation limits.

One high-severity issue was found and fixed: **exponential group membership expansion**. An ordinary signed-in member can use normal `POST /api/q` writes to create groups they manage with overlapping rules. A valid acyclic graph with two rules per level targeting the preceding group repeatedly recalculated each shared subtree. Queries, permission checks, background membership warming and rule previews could incur this work; it is outside the query visit budget. This gives an ordinary member a practical CPU/memory exhaustion path affecting all apps.

Before: overlapping group rules recursively recalculated shared groups, causing exponential allocation and CPU work.
Now: each calculation uses a shared membership resolver that reuses completed group results, preserving memberships, reasons, permissions and dependency tracking.

